\description{
\code{test_usage()} tests whether the share of a gene's expression carried by
each isoform differs between groups of samples, with one of three
established frameworks:
\itemize{
\item \code{"diffsplice"}: \code{edgeR} quasi-likelihood fits and \code{diffSplice}, the
pipeline of the 2025 \emph{Nucleic Acids Research} paper below. Fast, and the
one designed for counts divided by the read-ambiguity overdispersion.
\item \code{"satuRn"}: quasi-binomial models of transcript usage (Gilis et al.,
2021), suited to many samples and to single-cell data.
\item \code{"DEXSeq"}: negative binomial models of exon (here transcript) usage
(Anders et al. 2012), the slowest and often the most conservative.
}

Recent benchmarks of usage tests (2024 and 2025) rank these among the best;
none dominates, so the backend is an argument. With inferential
replicates in the object (see \code{\link[=add_uncertainty]{add_uncertainty()}}), the counts are divided
by the overdispersion that the read ambiguity adds (see
\code{\link[=inferential_overdispersion]{inferential_overdispersion()}}) before the test (\code{scale = TRUE}).

Transcripts are kept when at least \code{min_count} reads and a share of
\code{min_prop} of the gene fall on them in as many samples as the smallest
group, and genes need two such transcripts.

The gene-level p-value combines the transcript p-values of a gene with
Simes' method, and both levels are adjusted with the Benjamini-Hochberg
procedure within each contrast, so that the three backends report the same
columns.

With more than two groups, every contrast of \code{contrasts} is tested and the
results are stacked, one block per contrast. \code{"diffsplice"} and \code{"satuRn"}
fit all the groups once and test each contrast on that fit, so the
dispersions use every sample; \code{"DEXSeq"} compares a model with and without
a group effect, so it is fitted on the samples of the two groups of each
contrast.

\code{contrasts = "omnibus"} gives one p-value per transcript for a difference
among any of the groups, with \code{contrast = "omnibus"} and no fold-change
(\code{usage_log2FC} is \code{NA}; look at the contrasts for the direction).
\code{"DEXSeq"} compares its models with and without the group effect on all the
samples. \code{edgeR} and \code{satuRn} test one contrast at a time, so for them the
p-value is the Simes combination of the contrasts of every level against the
first: the groups can only differ if some level differs from the first, and
those contrasts share the reference, so they are positively correlated and
Simes' test keeps its error rate.
